  "bodyText": "Home\nVisit Us\nExhibitions\nEvents\nOnline Talks\nOxfordshire’s Military Heritage in 50 Objects\nCollections\nBlog\nResearch\nLearning\nShop\nCart\nMy Account\nAbout\nBecome a Friend\nSupport Us\nRoom Hire\n\n01993 810 210\n\nfrontofhouse@sofo.org.uk\n\n  \nHome\nEvents\nExhibitions\nCollections\nFriends’ Membership\nVisit\nShop\nBasket\nMy Account\nOxfordshire’s Military Heritage in 50 Objects\nResearch\nLearning\nOnline Talks\nBlog\nSupport Us\nRoom Hire\nImage Services\nAbout\nARNHEM AND THE OXFORDSHIRE & BUCKINGHAMSHIRE LIGHT INFANTRY\nOnline Talk and Live Q&A with Brigadier David Innes\n(FIRST STREAMED LIVE 19 JANUARY 2023)\n\nDONATE ONLINE NOW\n\nor donate by text!\n\nText ARNHEM followed by your donation amount to 70450 to give that amount (maximum donation £20 per text sent).\n\nE.g send a text with the message ARNHEM £5 to 70450 to make a donation of £5. Texts will cost the donation amount plus one standard network rate message.\n\nFind out more about Text Giving.\n\n\"A tale you will tell your grandchildren……”\n\nThis is the story of the men of the county regiment who fought and died at Arnhem in September 1944.\n\nAmong the soldiers of the Oxfordshire and Buckinghamshire Light Infantry that would serve at Arnhem with the Parachute Regiment, there is a Victoria Cross in this story and we shall meet the officer you will remember from the film ‘A Bridge too Far’ who was armed with an umbrella at Arnhem as well as other tales of courage, fortitude and sheer luck.\n\nTime too to salute the skill and daring of the men of the Glider Pilot Regiment, and the outstanding bravery of the local Dutch population who found themselves caught up in the aftermath of this battle.\n\nPlease consider making a donation online or by text. Your donations support the museum's work caring for its collections and sharing stories through events like these.\n\nMore on the Arnhem story - read Stephen Berridge's Arnhem Anniversary blog\nWHEN THE OXF AND BUCKS WENT “A BRIDGE TOO FAR”\nWHEN THE OXF AND BUCKS WENT “A BRIDGE TOO FAR” – 17TH SEPTEMBER 1944\nWHEN THE OXF AND BUCKS WENT “A BRIDGE TOO FAR” – 18TH SEPTEMBER 1944\nFrom the SOFO Store\n\nJOHN FROST SIGNED 1985 FIRST DAY COVER\n\nARNHEM AIRBORNE ASSAULT by Simon Smith (Unframed Print)\n\nSOFO Online Talks\nSearch for:\n\nSoldiers of Oxfordshire Museum\nPark Street.\nWoodstock\nOxfordshire OX20 1SN\n\nPhone and Email:\nGeneral Enquiries & Event Tickets: 01993 810 211\nfrontofhouse@sofo.org.uk\n\nPrivacy Policy\nTerms and conditions",
